Course.start({"num":19,"unit":4,"slug":"m19-standard-form-parallel","minutes":40,"calculator":"not needed","title":"Standard Form, Parallel and Perpendicular Lines","subtitle":"Ax + By = C, intercepts, horizontal and vertical lines, and slopes of parallel and perpendicular lines","lede":"City streets, floor tiles and graph paper are built from parallel and perpendicular lines. This week: a new form for linear equations, and how slopes tell you when lines never meet or meet at right angles.","recap":"To write a line from two points: find the slope, then use a point to find b. Point-slope form: y − y₁ = m(x − x₁).","goals":["Find intercepts and graph a line in standard form, Ax + By = C","Write and recognize horizontal and vertical lines","Use slopes to tell whether lines are parallel or perpendicular, and write their equations"],"context":{"title":"The grid that built Manhattan","place":"New York City · 1811","text":"In **1811**, New York City was a small town at the southern tip of Manhattan Island. North of it were farms, hills and marshes. The city's leaders made a bold plan for the future: the **Commissioners' Plan of 1811** laid out a giant grid of **12 avenues** running north and south and **155 streets** running east and west, crossing at **right angles**, over land where almost nobody lived yet.\n\nPeople laughed at it. Who would ever need streets all the way up to 155th Street? Within a century, the grid was full.\n\nIn math terms, every avenue is **parallel** to every other avenue: they never meet. Every street is **perpendicular** to every avenue: they meet at a 90° angle. The big exception is **Broadway**, an old road that cuts diagonally across the grid, creating famous triangle-shaped spaces like **Times Square**.\n\nTwice a year, the setting sun lines up exactly with the east-west streets, and New Yorkers crowd the crosswalks to photograph it. They call it **Manhattanhenge**.","ask":"How could you tell from two equations, without graphing them, whether their lines will ever cross?"},"learn":[{"title":"Standard form and intercepts","text":"**Standard form** of a linear equation is {{Ax + By = C}}, like {{2x + 3y = 12}}. It's handy for finding **intercepts**:\n\n- The **x-intercept** is where the line crosses the x-axis, so **y = 0**: {{2x = 12}}, {{x = 6}}. Point {{(6, 0)}}.\n- The **y-intercept** is where it crosses the y-axis, so **x = 0**: {{3y = 12}}, {{y = 4}}. Point {{(0, 4)}}.\n\nPlot those two points and draw the line. To find the slope, solve for y: {{y = -2/3 x + 4}}.","fig":"std"},{"title":"Horizontal and vertical lines","text":"- A **horizontal** line has the same y everywhere: {{y = 3}}. Slope **0**.\n- A **vertical** line has the same x everywhere: {{x = -2}}. Slope **undefined**.\n\nIt feels backwards at first: {{x = -2}} is the **vertical** line. Think: \"every point on it has x = −2,\" so it goes straight up and down through −2 on the x-axis.","fig":"hv","check":{"q":"Which is the equation of a **vertical** line through {{(5, -1)}}?","choices":[["{{x = 5}}",true,"Every point on it has x = 5."],["{{y = -1}}",false,"That's the horizontal line through the point."],["{{y = 5x}}",false]]}},{"title":"Parallel and perpendicular slopes","text":"- **Parallel** lines have the **same slope** (and different intercepts). {{y = 2x + 1}} and {{y = 2x - 4}} are parallel.\n- **Perpendicular** lines have slopes that are **negative reciprocals**: flip the fraction and change the sign. Their slopes multiply to **−1**. {{y = 2x + 1}} and {{y = -1/2 x + 3}} are perpendicular.","table":{"head":["Slope","Parallel slope","Perpendicular slope"],"rows":[["{{3}}","{{3}}","{{-1/3}}"],["{{-2/5}}","{{-2/5}}","{{5/2}}"],["{{1}}","{{1}}","{{-1}}"],["{{0}} (horizontal)","{{0}}","undefined (vertical)"]]},"worked":[{"problem":"Write the line **perpendicular** to {{y = 3x - 2}} through {{(6, 1)}}.","steps":[["{{m = -1/3}}","Negative reciprocal of 3."],["{{1 = -1/3(6) + b}}, so {{1 = -2 + b}}, {{b = 3}}"],["{{y = -1/3 x + 3}}"]]}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"cloze","title":"Find the intercepts","intro":"Set y = 0 for the x-intercept and x = 0 for the y-intercept.","items":["{{3x + 2y = 6}}: x-intercept [2], y-intercept [3]","{{4x - 5y = 20}}: x-intercept [5], y-intercept [-4]","{{x + 3y = -9}}: x-intercept [-9], y-intercept [-3]","{{-2x + y = 8}}: x-intercept [-4], y-intercept [8]","{{5x + 10y = 30}}: x-intercept [6], y-intercept [3]"],"hints":["x-intercept: cover up the y term (it's 0) and solve.","y-intercept: cover up the x term and solve."]},{"id":"t2","type":"plot","title":"Graph with intercepts","intro":"Plot the two intercepts.","items":[{"q":"Graph {{2x + 3y = 12}}.","fn":"-2x/3 + 4","need":2},{"q":"Graph {{x - 2y = 4}}.","fn":"x/2 - 2","need":2},{"q":"Graph the vertical line {{x = -3}}.","line":"x = -3","need":2},{"q":"Graph the horizontal line {{y = 2}}.","fn":"2","need":2}],"hints":["Find the x-intercept (y = 0) and y-intercept (x = 0), and plot both.","x = −3 is vertical: every point has x = −3."]},{"id":"t3","type":"sort","title":"Parallel, perpendicular or neither?","cats":["Parallel","Perpendicular","Neither"],"items":[["{{y = 4x + 1}} and {{y = 4x - 7}}",0],["{{y = 4x + 1}} and {{y = -1/4 x + 2}}",1,"4 × (−1/4) = −1."],["{{y = 4x + 1}} and {{y = -4x + 1}}",2,"−4 isn't the negative reciprocal of 4."],["{{y = 2/3 x}} and {{y = -3/2 x + 5}}",1],["{{y = 5}} and {{x = 2}}",1,"Horizontal and vertical."],["{{y = -x + 3}} and {{y = x + 3}}",1,"−1 × 1 = −1."],["{{y = 3}} and {{y = -1}}",0,"Both horizontal."],["{{y = 1/2 x + 1}} and {{y = 2x + 1}}",2,"1/2 × 2 = 1, not −1."]],"hints":["Same slope → parallel. Slopes multiply to −1 → perpendicular.","Horizontal and vertical lines are always perpendicular."]},{"id":"t4","type":"solve","title":"Write the parallel or perpendicular line","pick":4,"intro":"Type the equation in slope-intercept form, like **y = 2x - 5**.","items":[{"q":"**Parallel** to {{y = 2x + 3}}, through {{(1, -3)}}","a":"y = 2x - 5","how":["m = 2","{{-3 = 2(1) + b}}, {{b = -5}}"]},{"q":"**Perpendicular** to {{y = 2x + 3}}, through {{(4, 1)}}","a":"y = -1/2x + 3","how":["m = −1/2","{{1 = -1/2(4) + b = -2 + b}}, {{b = 3}}"]},{"q":"**Parallel** to {{y = -3x}}, through {{(-2, 4)}}","a":"y = -3x - 2","how":["m = −3","{{4 = -3(-2) + b = 6 + b}}, {{b = -2}}"]},{"q":"**Perpendicular** to {{y = 3/4 x - 1}}, through the origin","a":"y = -4/3x","how":["m = −4/3, b = 0"]},{"q":"**Perpendicular** to {{y = -x + 6}}, through {{(2, 5)}}","a":"y = x + 3","how":["m = 1","{{5 = 2 + b}}, {{b = 3}}"]},{"q":"**Parallel** to {{y = 7}}, through {{(3, -2)}}","a":"y = -2","how":["Horizontal: **y = −2**"]}],"hints":["Parallel: copy the slope. Perpendicular: flip it and change the sign.","Then use the point to find b."]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Standard form to slope-intercept","pick":4,"intro":"Solve for y. Type the equation, like **y = -2x + 4**.","items":[{"q":"{{2x + y = 4}}","a":"y = -2x + 4","how":["Subtract 2x: **y = −2x + 4**"]},{"q":"{{3x + 3y = 9}}","a":"y = -x + 3","how":["{{3y = -3x + 9}}","**y = −x + 3**"]},{"q":"{{x - 2y = 6}}","a":"y = 1/2x - 3","how":["{{-2y = -x + 6}}","Divide by −2: **y = (1/2)x − 3**"]},{"q":"{{4x + 5y = 10}}","a":"y = -4/5x + 2","how":["{{5y = -4x + 10}}","**y = −(4/5)x + 2**"]},{"q":"{{-6x + 2y = 8}}","a":"y = 3x + 4","how":["{{2y = 6x + 8}}","**y = 3x + 4**"]}],"hints":["Move the x term to the right side. Then divide everything by the coefficient of y."]},{"id":"t6","type":"quiz","title":"Lines on the grid","fig":"manhattan","intro":"Here's a simplified street map. Streets run left-right; avenues run up-down. The dashed line is Broadway.","questions":[{"q":"The street through {{(0, 3)}} has which equation?","choices":[["{{y = 3}}",true],["{{x = 3}}",false,"Streets run left to right: horizontal."],["{{y = 3x}}",false]]},{"q":"The avenue through {{(-2, 0)}} has which equation?","choices":[["{{x = -2}}",true],["{{y = -2}}",false,"Avenues run up and down: vertical."],["{{y = -2x}}",false]]},{"q":"Broadway passes through {{(-4, -2)}} and {{(4, 2)}}. What's its slope?","choices":[["{{1/2}}",true,"4/8 = 1/2."],["{{2}}",false],["{{-1/2}}",false]]},{"q":"A new road crosses Broadway at a right angle. What's its slope?","choices":[["{{-2}}",true,"The negative reciprocal of 1/2."],["{{1/2}}",false,"That's parallel."],["{{2}}",false,"Flip it, but also change the sign."]]}]}],"build":{"heading":"Design a street map","prompt":"Design a small town on a coordinate grid. Give the equations of: **two parallel roads**, a **road perpendicular** to them, and **one horizontal** and **one vertical** street. Explain how you know each pair is parallel or perpendicular, using their slopes.","model":"Main Road is y = 2x + 1 and River Road is y = 2x - 5. They're parallel because both have slope 2. School Lane is y = -1/2x + 4. It's perpendicular to both because 2 × (-1/2) = -1. First Street is the horizontal line y = 3, and Park Avenue is the vertical line x = -2.","placeholder":"Main Road is y = …","reqs":[{"t":"At least 3 slanted lines in y = mx + b form","re":"y\\s*=\\s*[-−]?\\d*\\.?\\d*(/\\d+)?\\s*x","min":3},{"t":"A horizontal line (y = a number)","re":"\\by\\s*=\\s*[-−]?\\d+\\.?\\d*(?![\\d./]*\\s*x)\\b"},{"t":"A vertical line (x = a number)","re":"\\bx\\s*=\\s*[-−]?\\d"},{"t":"Use the words *parallel* and *perpendicular*","re":"\\b(parallel|perpendicular)\\b","min":2}]},"reflect":{"q":"The Manhattan grid was planned for a city that didn't exist yet. People thought it was silly to plan so far ahead. What's something you're doing now (in school or anywhere) that's really a plan for your future self?","placeholder":"Something I'm doing now for my future is…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Find the missing value","items":[{"q":"For what {{k}} are {{y = kx + 1}} and {{y = 5x - 3}} parallel?","a":"5","pre":"k =","how":["Same slope: **k = 5**"]},{"q":"For what {{k}} are {{y = kx + 1}} and {{y = 5x - 3}} perpendicular?","a":"-1/5","pre":"k =","how":["Negative reciprocal: **−1/5**"]},{"q":"For what {{a}} is {{ax + 2y = 7}} parallel to {{y = 3x}}?","a":"-6","pre":"a =","how":["{{2y = -ax + 7}}, slope {{-a/2}}","{{-a/2 = 3}}, **a = −6**"]},{"q":"Write the line perpendicular to {{2x - 3y = 6}} through {{(2, -1)}}.","a":"y = -3/2x + 2","how":["Slope of 2x − 3y = 6 is 2/3. Perpendicular: −3/2.","{{-1 = -3/2(2) + b = -3 + b}}, {{b = 2}}"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"quiz","title":"Is it a rectangle?","intro":"A shape has corners {{A(0, 0)}}, {{B(4, 2)}}, {{C(3, 4)}} and {{D(-1, 2)}}.","questions":[{"q":"What's the slope of side AB?","choices":[["{{1/2}}",true],["{{2}}",false],["{{-2}}",false]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"What's the slope of side BC?","choices":[["{{-2}}",true,"(4 − 2)/(3 − 4) = 2/(−1)."],["{{2}}",false],["{{-1/2}}",false]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"So AB and BC are…","choices":[["perpendicular",true,"1/2 × (−2) = −1."],["parallel",false],["neither",false]],"keepOrder":true},{"q":"Check CD and DA too. Is ABCD a rectangle?","choices":[["Yes: opposite sides are parallel and all corners are right angles",true,"CD has slope 1/2 and DA has slope −2."],["No",false]],"keepOrder":true}]}],"figs":{"std":{"x":[-2,8],"y":[-2,6],"w":260,"lines":[{"fn":"-2x/3 + 4","label":"2x + 3y = 12"}],"points":[[6,0,"(6, 0)"],[0,4,"(0, 4)"]],"alt":"The line 2x + 3y = 12 through its intercepts (6, 0) and (0, 4)"},"hv":{"x":[-5,5],"y":[-3,5],"w":240,"lines":[{"fn":"3","label":"y = 3"},{"xeq":-2,"c":1}],"texts":[[-3.2,-2.2,"x = −2"]],"alt":"A horizontal line y = 3 and a vertical line x = −2"},"manhattan":{"x":[-6,6],"y":[-5,5],"w":260,"lines":[{"fn":"3","c":1},{"fn":"0","c":1},{"fn":"-3","c":1},{"xeq":-2,"c":0},{"xeq":2,"c":0},{"fn":"x/2","c":2,"dash":true}],"points":[[-4,-2],[4,2]],"alt":"Horizontal streets, vertical avenues, and a diagonal dashed line for Broadway"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** Manhattanhenge happens around May 28–30 and July 11–13 each year, when sunset lines up with the streets. The grid is tilted about 29° from true east-west, which is why it doesn't happen on the equinoxes.","unitTitle":"Linear Functions","code":"MTE4MS1OQVRUQUhOQU0=","star":"ODU2Ni1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m18-writing-linear-equations.html","next":"m20-scatter-plots.html"});
